@page "/exgrid/selection"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Selection and keyboard"
          Lead="Selection works as it does in Excel: rectangles, a Focus that stays put while an Extent moves, and Enter and Tab that cycle inside what you selected. Selecting is cheap, so it is never capped.">

    <DocsSection Title="Rectangles, a Focus and an Extent">
        <p>
            A selection is one or more rectangles of cells. The <strong>Focus</strong> is Excel's active cell: the
            cell typing goes into, outlined when it is selected alone, left unshaded inside a selected range, and
            given a thin border of its own among several ranges. The <strong>Extent</strong> is the corner that
            moves while you extend a range.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Click</strong> selects one cell. <strong>Drag</strong> or <strong>Shift+click</strong> draws a rectangle from the Focus.</li>
            <li><strong>Ctrl+click</strong> adds another rectangle. On a cell that is already selected, it takes that cell out.</li>
            <li>Clicking a <strong>header</strong> sorts. <strong>Shift+click</strong> on a header selects whole columns, and <strong>Ctrl+Space</strong> does from the keyboard.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The <strong>Heading Mark</strong> shows where the selection is: each column's header and each Row
            Heading, where present, is shaded and its letters coloured when its column or row holds part of the
            selection. A line two device pixels thick runs along its edge toward the cells, unbroken across
            neighbouring marked Headings. A whole column or row takes a distinct shade, even when several ranges
            together cover it; overlapping ranges do not make up for an unselected cell. Where the grid has a
            Headings corner, its triangle takes the mark's colour when every cell is selected.
        </p>
        <p>
            The mark changes without rendering the data row components again. Its line, letters and selected corner triangle use
            <code>--ex-heading-mark</code>, the Focus outline's colour unless you set it. The shades use
            <code>--ex-heading-mark-background</code> and <code>--ex-heading-mark-whole-background</code>
            (<a href="exgrid/appearance#visual-tokens">Visual Tokens</a>). Where the header or a Row Heading covers
            a side of the Focus or a single range, the mark supplies its two device pixels without a second outline
            beside it. Against a pinned block, or a grid edge without a Heading, the outline stays inside.
            Select a range in the example below, then Ctrl+click elsewhere: the columns between the two ranges
            stay unmarked.
            The ordinary Focus and single-range outline defaults to two device pixels on every side,
            including at display scales above 100%.
        </p>
        <p>
            Positions are in the current order. When the order of the rows changes — a sort, a filter, a row
            inserted — the same positions would name other rows, so the grid <strong>drops the selection</strong>
            rather than leave it on rows you did not choose.
        </p>
        <p>
            Keys typed against the dropped selection that were still on their way when the order changed go with
            it: typing opens no editor and writes nothing, and the grid says so through <code>OnEditDiscarded</code>,
            once for the whole run of keys; an arrow moves nothing. A key pressed once the new order is on screen
            places the Focus on the first cell in view, as the first key on an empty selection always does.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Reading the selection" Builtin="typeof(SelectionStatus)" Mud="typeof(SelectionStatusMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])">
            <code>SelectionChanged</code> hands over a <code>GridSelection</code>: its ranges, the Focus, and the cell
            count. Select one cell, then Shift+click another to compare the marked headers. Press Ctrl+Space to
            select the whole column and see its different shade, then Ctrl+A to select every cell. Try Ctrl+click,
            then Ctrl+Shift+↓, and compare both Chromes.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The keyboard">
        <p>Click into the grid, or tab to it: the grid is one tab stop, and its keys are Excel's.</p>
        <p>
            With Narrator, turn scan mode off before you use the grid's keys: the Narrator key and Space
            (Insert+Space). In scan mode the arrow keys move Narrator's own reading cursor through the page and
            do not reach the grid, and Narrator turns scan mode on again on each new page. That is what Narrator
            was seen to do; the grid itself is checked against the browser's accessibility tree, not against a
            screen reader.
        </p>
        <p>
            A screen reader is given the grid as a table. The header row is its row 1 and the first row of data its
            row 2, and the grid writes each row's number the same way near the top and a million rows down. Each
            tier of Header Groups is a row of its own above the header row, and the rows of data are counted after
            them; with <code>HideHeader</code>, the first row of data is row 1.
        </p>
        @* The rows the Keyboard reference lists for this section, so the two are one list (ADR-0110). *@
        <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table">
            <thead><tr><th style="width: 34%">Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in Keyboard.ExplainedAt("exgrid/selection#the-keyboard"))
                {
                    <tr><td><KeyText Text="@row.Keys" /></td><td><KeyText Text="@row.What" /></td></tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </MudSimpleTable>
        <p>
            <strong>Why Enter and Tab never leave a range:</strong> select five cells and type five values, pressing
            Enter after each. The fifth Enter returns to the first cell instead of carrying on into a sixth row you
            did not mean to touch. Excel's "edge of the data block" for Ctrl+arrow is not used: query results have
            no blank rows, and finding a block's edge would need every row.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Keys typed at once arrive in order.</strong> Over Blazor Server a popover opens or closes a round
            trip after the key or the press that asked, and the keys typed meanwhile wait and then go where the
            keyboard lands: ↓, ↓ typed straight after Escape in a column's menu, or after Enter on a filter panel's OK,
            move the Focus two rows, rather than moving in a menu that is going or being lost on a button.
        </p>
        <p>
            Every key of the three products, each with the page that explains it, is in the
            <a href="reference/keyboard">Keyboard reference</a>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The page follows the Focus">
        <p>
            A move the grid makes — the arrows, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + arrow, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd>,
            <kbd>PageUp</kbd> and <kbd>PageDown</kbd>, <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Tab</kbd>, and
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Backspace</kbd> — first brings the cell into the grid's own Viewport, then asks the
            browser to bring it into the window, at the nearest edge: on a grid taller than the window, the page
            scrolls as the Focus walks past the fold, as a spreadsheet's window does. While a range is extended it
            is the far end that is followed, however far it has carried the Viewport from the Focus. Every other key
            that the grid answers by bringing a cell into its Viewport is followed the same way — <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> +
            <kbd>A</kbd>, <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Space</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Space</kbd>, and
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>.</kbd> among them — so what the grid shows and what the window shows never
            part. Opening the editor follows the same rule, so a cell is followed the same way whether it is reached
            or edited. A cell already in the window moves nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
            Nothing is followed once the keyboard has left the grid, or once the Focus has moved on, and a resize, or
            a panel opening beside the grid, never moves the page: only a move does.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>A page with a fixed bar sets <code>scroll-padding</code></strong> on its scrolling element, so that
            a cell is not brought in under the bar: the browser places it, and the bar is the page's to know about.
            This site sets <code>html { scroll-padding-top: … }</code> to its app bar's height.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Driving the grid from a script">
        <p>
            Tools drive a grid at machine speed — a Playwright test, an RPA script, a user's own macro — and on
            Blazor Server every key waits a round trip for its answer. A script that reads the grid straight after
            typing reads it before the grid has finished. So the grid says when it has, with its Settled Mark: its root carries
            <code>data-ex-settled</code> while every input it has taken has been handled and its effect is on the
            page. The attribute goes the moment a key, a press on the rows, a click on one of the grid's own controls,
            a paste, a composition or a copy is taken, and comes back once nothing is held, nothing is owed, and a cell brought into view has been
            painted there. It changes once as a burst of input starts and once as it ends, and nothing else moves
            with it: the rows do not render for it.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Type at full speed, then wait for the mark, then read.</strong> With Playwright that is
            <code>await expect(page.locator('.ex-grid')).toHaveAttribute('data-ex-settled', '')</code> between
            the last input and the first reading. Never wait a fixed time between inputs to make a script pass: a
            user typing quickly meets the very ordering the wait hides, and the grid already keeps every input in
            its place.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Clicks keep their place among the keys.</strong> A click on a heading, a column's ▾, an item of the
            column menu or the Context Menu, a completion candidate, the pager, the status line's buttons, a grip's
            double click (Size to fit), a filter panel's OK and Cancel, and Find's Previous and Next is numbered as it
            happens and taken in its turn: after every key typed before it, before every key typed after it, however
            long the grid takes to answer either. A click made while keys are still being answered waits with them and
            is carried out in its turn, so <code>Enter</code> then a click on ▾, sent at once, opens the menu of the row
            the Enter moved to, as it does typed slowly.
        </p>
        <p>
            The mark covers what the grid's own keyboard and pointer handling takes. Text typed into a field — the Cell
            Editor, a Sheet's Formula Bar or Name Box — and a click on a control a substituted Chrome draws, a Sheet's
            toolbar or a Pivot's field list reach the grid as ordinary Blazor events: read their effect with an
            expectation that waits for it. It is not <code>aria-busy</code>, which a screen reader would read out.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Status-bar figures">
        <p>
            Select two cells or more and the status line under the grid shows what Excel's status bar shows:
            <strong>Average, Count and Sum</strong>. Right-click the line to choose among all six — Average,
            Count, Numerical Count, Min, Max and Sum. The figures cover <strong>every selected cell</strong>, the
            rows not painted and not yet fetched included: Ctrl+Shift+↓ down a column of a million rows sums a
            million rows.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Who sums.</strong> The grid does not hold your data, so it asks, as it does for
                <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>. <code>GridSource.From</code> answers by itself; a server answers
                through <code>GridSource.Fetch(..., summarize: ...)</code>; a grid you push rows to answers through
                <code>OnSummarize</code>. With nobody to ask, no figure is shown.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>What counts.</strong> A number is in every figure, summed exactly when it is an integer or a
                <code>decimal</code>. Text, a Boolean and a date are counted and never summed. A blank is in none.
                A cell under two ranges counts once. An answerer that holds error values — a Sheet, a pivot report,
                your own through <code>GridSummaryCells</code> — counts an error in Count and never in Numerical
                Count, and Average, Min, Max and Sum are then shown nowhere, as in Excel. Those four wear the format of
                the Focus's column, as Excel's wear the active cell's.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Never the wrong total.</strong> Change the selection and the figures go at once; they come
                back only as the answer to the selection you now have. A selection the server will not sum shows
                its reason, never part of a total.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>On by default.</strong> Any grid that can summarise shows the line, so that selecting does
                not move the rows under your hand. <code>ShowSelectionSummary="false"</code> takes it off the
                grid; <code>OnSelectionSummaryChanged</code> still hands you the figures, for a status bar of your own.
                Bind <code>SummaryFigures</code> to keep the menu's choice; without a binding the menu is not offered.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Title="Status-bar figures" Builtin="typeof(SelectionFigures)" Mud="typeof(SelectionFiguresMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])">
            Select some Notional cells, then right-click the line under the grid. <code>OnSelectionSummaryChanged</code>
            hands the same figures to a status bar of your own, shown under the grid.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Which row am I on?">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>HighlightFocusRow</code> paints a band across the row holding the Focus — Excel 365's Focus
                Cell — which helps on a grid wider than the screen.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>HighlightHoverRow</code> paints a band under the pointer, what design systems call
                <em>hover</em>. On a <code>MudExGridPaper</code>, <code>Hover="true"</code> turns it on.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Both are drawn by an overlay, not by the rows, so moving them renders no row. Their colours are the
            Visual Tokens <code>--ex-focus-row-fill</code> and <code>--ex-row-hover-background</code>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

